{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778940832,"nonce":"uLRREd6lMzk1TddvciTsx9gaF80=","message":"## Addendum: distributed and multi-machine ledger strategy\n\nThe ledger should be designed so Mandible can evolve through three deployment/sync modes without a backend rewrite. The important boundary is that SQLite is the local storage engine, not the conceptual backend. The conceptual backend is an append-only Mandible ledger with adapter-specific import/export and conflict handling.\n\n### Phase 1: local SQLite ledger plus git-bug compatibility mirror\n\nMandible owns the hot path locally:\n\n- issue/comment/label/status/relationship/work-unit/auth/session mutations go through the ledger service;\n- every mutation writes current-state rows plus append-only ledger events/outbox records;\n- UI, graph, CLI, auth merge handling, and issue detail read from the ledger, not from git-bug;\n- git-bug is an adapter that imports/exports asynchronously with visible sync/conflict state.\n\nThe git-bug mirror should be as useful and durable as possible, but it should not be treated as a perfect natural representation of Mandible's full model.\n\nGit-bug can naturally represent:\n\n- issue title/body;\n- open/closed status;\n- labels;\n- comments;\n- authors/timestamps when available;\n- some issue relationships through labels or structured comments.\n\nMandible-specific state does not naturally fit git-bug:\n\n- work-unit lifecycle;\n- auth request links and merge outcomes;\n- session links, live/idle session state, and parent/child agent sessions;\n- typed relationship provenance/confidence;\n- event outbox/sync metadata;\n- graph display metadata;\n- provider/artifact records.\n\nFor git-bug export, we should still pack as much structured information as reasonably belongs there, especially where it remains human-readable and useful outside Mandible. Recommended conventions:\n\n- keep normal issue content human-readable;\n- use namespaced labels for stable issue facts where labels fit well, e.g. `mandible:kind:umbrella`, `mandible:priority:high`, `mandible:parent:\u003cid\u003e`, `mandible:blocked-by:\u003cid\u003e`, `mandible:related:\u003cid\u003e`;\n- preserve existing compatibility labels such as `parent:\u003cid\u003e` during migration when they already exist;\n- use deterministic structured comment/trailer blocks only for metadata that labels cannot carry cleanly;\n- avoid dumping noisy live/runtime state into git-bug unless it has durable historical value.\n\nThe round-trip target for phase 1 should be explicit:\n\n- lossless for core issue fields: title, body, status, labels, comments, basic issue relationships, aliases/provenance where git-bug exposes them;\n- metadata-assisted or best-effort for Mandible-specific operational records;\n- not the only lossless backup for the full ledger.\n\nThat means Mandible DB backup/export has to be updated as part of ledger work. Existing backup infrastructure must include the ledger tables, event/outbox tables, adapter cursors, conflict records, and any canonical link tables.\n\n### Phase 2: Mandible-native distributed git refs\n\nIf we need a lossless repo-portable representation, the right next step is a Mandible-native git-ref transport, not trying to force all ledger semantics into git-bug.\n\nPossible shape:\n\n- store/replay append-only ledger events in refs such as `refs/mandible/ledger/*`;\n- keep event IDs globally stable and content-address or hash event payloads for integrity;\n- sync those refs through normal git fetch/push;\n- treat SQLite as a local projection/cache rebuilt from the event log when needed;\n- keep git-bug export as compatibility for humans/tools that only understand git-bug.\n\nThis phase should be mostly a new adapter/transport if phase 1 is built correctly. It should not require changing the issue/comment/work-unit/auth/session domain model.\n\n### Phase 3: central Mandible service\n\nA central service can come later if real-time multi-user collaboration, permissions, web access, or shared operational state becomes important.\n\nIn that model:\n\n- the central service accepts the same ledger commands/events;\n- local SQLite can become a cache/replica/offline queue;\n- adapter cursors and conflict records still apply;\n- git-bug and Mandible-native git refs can remain export/import transports rather than the main collaboration mechanism.\n\nThis should be a deployment/sync change, not a new product model, if the ledger API is designed as command/event based from the beginning.\n\n### Practical multi-machine behavior\n\nFor another machine working on the same project in phase 1:\n\n1. It fetches/clones the repo and its git-bug refs.\n2. Mandible imports git-bug issues into the local ledger.\n3. Core issues/comments/labels/status/relationships appear locally.\n4. Mandible-specific records appear only if they were represented in namespaced labels or structured metadata blocks, or restored from a Mandible backup/export.\n\nFor a fork, Mandible needs an explicit logical project identity model:\n\n- local checkout path is not enough;\n- multiple local worktrees/clones of the same remote should resolve to the same logical project identity;\n- forks should be configurable as either continuing the upstream issue namespace or starting a distinct project namespace.\n\nThis implies a `project_id` or `remote_identity` layer separate from current `work_repos.root` path identity.\n\n### Design constraints this adds to implementation\n\n- All mutations must go through a ledger command API; routes should not write raw SQL or git-bug directly.\n- `ledger_events` must be complete enough to drive future Mandible-native refs or central-service replication.\n- Sync adapters need cursors/version state per external system, not only dirty flags on issues.\n- Conflicts need first-class records and UI actions: keep Mandible, accept external, or manually merge.\n- Canonical IDs must be globally stable and not derived from local paths.\n- Core canonical links should use strict tables where possible; graph edges can remain projections.\n- git-bug export should be useful and human-readable, but not overloaded with volatile runtime state.\n\n### Recommended conclusion\n\nPhase 1 should still proceed with SQLite as the local canonical store and git-bug as a first-class compatibility mirror. The important correction is that git-bug is not the only full-fidelity durability story. We should preserve as much as cleanly fits in git-bug, especially namespaced relationship labels, while designing the ledger event model so a later Mandible-native git-ref transport can provide lossless distributed sync without another backend rewrite.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}